<tr id="S6.T1.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T1.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">1900</th>
<td id="S6.T1.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.44</td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.45</td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.50</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Comparison of accuracy across different subset selection methods</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The results demonstrate that both DPP and our proposed method generally outperform random selection, particularly at smaller subset sizes, highlighting the importance of strategic data selection for efficient fine-tuning. The DPP approach shows particularly strong performance at the 900 example budget (0.49 accuracy), outperforming both random selection (0.41) and our method (0.42) at this budget level. However, our method shows competitive performance at the 1000 example level with 0.47 accuracy, suggesting that different selection strategies may be optimal at different budget constraints.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.5 </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S6.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For evaluation of the performance the metrics that helps are given as follows:
<span id="S6.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy</span>, the percentage of correctly solved problems on test sets. <span id="S6.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reasoning Quality</span>, to assess the quality of the generated reasoning steps, we are using ROUGE and BERTScore.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
